// Blade plot module, v1 (plotly-only).
//
// `import plot` gives the plotting factories; each one serializes a plotly
// figure (application/vnd.plotly.v1+json) and hands it to `display.emit`,
// which writes ONE display frame per call (Blade-REPL/docs/display-frames.md).
// The editor's plot panel renders the payload; nothing here knows how.
//
// FACTORY SHAPE. Every factory takes its REQUIRED data arrays positionally,
// then defaulted OPTION SLOTS, each tagged with its own option-marker
// quantity below. Slots fill by NOMINAL, in any order, flat or chained --
//
//     plot.contourf(x, y, z)
//     plot.contourf(x, y, z, 20: levels, "waves": title)
//     plot.contourf(x, y, z)("waves": title)(1: cmap)
//
// -- with duplicate slots, unknown tags, and ambiguous mixes rejected
// (BL3014), and a bare value in a slot position rejected until ascribed
// (BL3010). Omitted slots take the defaults declared here.
//
// STRUCTURE NOTE. The array serialization (d.json_array / d.json_num) is
// written INLINE in each factory rather than in a shared generic helper for
// the RANK-1 factories (line/scatter), which take abstract-rank parameters.
// The GRID factories go through the shared `__axis_json` / `__grid_json`
// helpers below: a module-exported generic function whose body calls ANOTHER
// generic function does monomorphize today (pinned by
// tests/corpus/multifile/014_cross_module_generic_function), which is what
// makes the decimation ladder shareable across the three of them.
//
// COLORMAPS, v1: an Int index into a fixed table (0 = Viridis default,
// 1 = Plasma, 2 = Cividis, 3 = Greys, 4 = RdBu, 5 = Depth). 0-4 are plotly
// NAMED scales; 5 is an inline colorscale ARRAY (black -> navy -> blue ->
// pale blue -> cream), which is why __cmap_json exists: the trace needs a JSON
// VALUE either way, and for 0-4 that value is the quoted name -- byte-
// identical to what the module emitted when only names existed. An
// EnumIdx<["Viridis",...]> upgrade would make the selection nominal AND
// checked; deferred.
//
// PREFERRED BACKEND (the `backend` slot, all five factories). A program can
// say which renderer it would LIKE the viewer to use -- `1: backend` for GR
// -- without changing one byte of what it emits. This is a HINT and nothing
// more:
//
//   * the frame is still a plotly figure, with the same mime, the same
//     payload, and `meta.backend` still reading `"plotly"`. `meta.backend`
//     names the backend that PRODUCED the render, and a viewer caches its
//     per-plot renders under that key -- stamping `"gr"` on plotly JSON
//     would file it as though it WERE the GR render and permanently
//     suppress the real one. The preference is therefore a SEPARATE,
//     ADDITIVE key:
//
//         {"id":"blade-1","backend":"plotly","preferredBackend":"gr"}
//
//   * a viewer with no GR renderer -- or no knowledge of the key at all --
//     ignores it and paints the plotly figure, which is the whole reason
//     the frame content does not move;
//   * the key is emitted ONLY when the program asks for a non-default
//     backend. Left at its default, the meta is BYTE-IDENTICAL to what this
//     module emitted before the slot existed, which is what keeps the
//     existing frame byte pins honest;
//   * out-of-table values fall back to the default rather than erroring,
//     exactly like `cmap`: a backend choice is presentation, not data.
//
// The two metas are separate string LITERALS chosen by a runtime `if` in
// `__emit_fig`, because `display.emit`'s meta argument must be a literal
// (BL5700, "the meta argument must be a string literal") -- the frame's head
// and meta tail are computed once at elaboration time, so a meta built by
// concatenation is refused even when both halves are literals.
//
// VALID JSON IS AN INVARIANT, not an aspiration: every runtime value that
// reaches a figure goes through a serializer that cannot emit invalid JSON.
// Title/label strings travel via `d.json_string` (which supplies its OWN
// quotes -- never wrap it in more), and NaN/Inf array elements serialize as
// `null`, which is what plotly's own encoder writes.
//
// DECIMATION (grid factories only: contourf / contour / heatmap). A plotly
// figure costs roughly 20 bytes per sample, so a raw 2000x2000 z grid is
// ~80 MB of JSON -- over the display frame's 32 MB hard cap, and far past
// what a panel that paints at most ~1600 px can use. The `maxdim` slot caps
// the axis length that reaches the panel:
//
//     BOTH axes within the cap  ->  x, y and z are serialized UNCHANGED.
//                                   Byte-for-byte the frame this module
//                                   emitted before decimation existed; the
//                                   existing frame pins are the guard.
//     EITHER axis over the cap  ->  both axes are resampled to a BUDGET of
//                                   B points, index k reading source index
//                                   (k * n) / B. x, y and z are resampled
//                                   together, so the trace stays rectangular.
//
// LIMITS of the v1 rule, all deliberate and all visible in the output:
//
//   * THE BUDGET IS A POWER OF TWO, not `maxdim` itself. `maxdim` is the
//     TRIGGER exactly as written -- an axis is left alone until it exceeds
//     it -- but the length it is resampled TO is B, the largest of
//     16/32/64/128/256/512 that is <= `maxdim` (16 when `maxdim` is under
//     16, and never more than 512 however large `maxdim` is). An array's
//     extent is part of its TYPE in Blade, so a decimated axis has to have a
//     length the compiler knows, and a length computed from a runtime
//     `maxdim` is not a type. One helper per budget, picked by a runtime
//     `if`, is how a runtime cap reaches a static extent.
//   * THE LAST SAMPLE FALLS SHORT OF THE DATA EDGE. Index k reads
//     (k * n) / B, so the last one is ((B-1) * n) / B, not n - 1: a
//     decimated axis stops up to one stride before the true maximum. This is
//     accepted v1 behavior -- it keeps every sample an ORIGINAL datum at an
//     evenly strided position (no interpolation, no duplicated endpoint),
//     which is what makes the decimated frame honest about what was
//     measured.
//   * BOTH AXES SHARE ONE DECISION. A grid over the cap on one axis only
//     (say 4000 x 12) is resampled on BOTH, so the short axis repeats
//     samples. The frame is then bounded by B*B samples whatever the input
//     shape, which is the property that keeps the cap a cap.
//   * A DECIMATED SAMPLE KEEPS ITS SOURCE TYPE. An integer grid serializes
//     as integer JSON on BOTH paths, and a Float32 grid stays Float32:
//     resampling selects original data, it does not convert it.
module plot

import display as d

// ---- Option-marker quantities (dimensionless: the nominal IS the content) ----

/// Contour count for contour/contourf (plotly `ncontours`).
Unit levels: 1
/// Colormap table index (see the table in `__cmap_name`).
Unit cmap: 1
/// Figure title (tags Strings).
Unit title: 1
/// X-axis label (tags Strings).
Unit xlabel: 1
/// Y-axis label (tags Strings).
Unit ylabel: 1
/// Longest grid axis, in samples, that reaches the panel undecimated
/// (grid factories only -- see the DECIMATION note in the module header).
Unit maxdim: 1
/// Lower bound of a FIXED color range (grid factories). Inert until zmax is
/// given -- see the zmax marker below.
Unit zmin: 1
/// Upper bound of a FIXED color range (grid factories). A non-negative zmax
/// ACTIVATES the pair: the trace gains `"zauto":false,"zmin":L,"zmax":H` and
/// a viewer colors (and levels) the grid against [zmin, zmax] instead of the
/// frame's own data range -- which is what keeps a color scale steady across
/// frames of an animation. Left at its default (-1, meaning "automatic"),
/// the trace is BYTE-IDENTICAL to what this module emitted before the pair
/// existed, the same compatibility rule the `backend` slot follows. The
/// activation test is `zmax >= 0`, so an all-negative fixed range is not
/// expressible in v1 (rain rates, spectra and moduli do not miss it).
Unit zmax: 1
/// Epoch (or any outer-loop) index a `plot.stream` frame belongs to. -1 is
/// "unmarked" -- the default, and what a stream with no outer loop sends. A
/// non-negative value lets the renderer draw the boundary between one epoch's
/// batches and the next without the program having to say where they are.
Unit epoch: 1
/// Smooth-rendering mode (contourf and heatmap, v1): 0 (default) fills discrete
/// bands between contour levels -- the classic filled-contour look, and a
/// STEPPED colorbar; 1 colors the field as a CONTINUOUS gradient with the
/// contour lines drawn on top, and the colorbar renders as a smooth ramp
/// (plotly "contours.coloring":"heatmap"), with the contour LINES omitted
/// entirely -- plotly strokes them across the full axis range, which leaks
/// lines outside the data rectangle whenever the view is zoomed out past
/// the data. Out-of-table values take the
/// default, like cmap; at the default the trace is byte-identical to the
/// pre-slot module.
Unit smoothing: 1
/// Renderer the program would PREFER the viewer paint this figure with:
/// 0 = plotly (the default), 1 = GR. Out-of-table indices fall back to the
/// default rather than erroring, like `cmap`. This is a HINT carried in the
/// frame's `meta.preferredBackend`, never a change to the frame's CONTENT --
/// see the PREFERRED BACKEND note in the module header.
Unit backend: 1
/// Stable identity for a figure that a later evaluation will REPLACE rather
/// than sit beside (heatmap and contourf, v1). Non-empty routes the frame through
/// `display.emit_id` -- the same primitive `stream` uses -- so every frame
/// sharing the id merges into ONE panel entry, exactly as stream instalments
/// do. Left empty (the default), the frame takes the ordinary per-emit
/// ordinal id and the emitted bytes are IDENTICAL to the pre-slot module;
/// like `stream`, an id'd emit consumes NO ordinal, so the plots around it
/// keep the ids they always had.
Unit plotid: 1
/// Camera contract for a RE-EVALUATABLE view (heatmap and contourf, v1): the names
/// of the session bindings that position this figure, comma-separated --
/// e.g. "cam_cx,cam_cy,cam_r" for center-x, center-y, half-width, in that
/// order. Carried in the figure's LAYOUT (`"blade_camera":{"bindings":...}`),
/// never in the frame meta: `display.emit`'s meta must be a string literal
/// (BL5700), while the layout is runtime-built JSON, which is exactly what a
/// runtime contract needs. A viewer that understands the key can map a zoom
/// gesture back onto those bindings and re-evaluate; a viewer that does not
/// simply ignores an extra layout entry. Empty (the default) emits no entry,
/// keeping the layout byte-identical to the pre-slot module.
Unit camera: 1

// ---- Decimation budgets ----
//
// One index type per supported budget. These are the only lengths a
// decimated axis can have, because a Blade array's extent lives in its TYPE:
// `range<__B512>` iterates a compile-time 512, while the STRIDE it reads
// with -- `(k * n) / 512` -- is an ordinary runtime expression. That split is
// the whole trick, and it is why the ladder exists instead of a single
// helper parameterized by the cap.

type __B16 = Idx<16>
type __B32 = Idx<32>
type __B64 = Idx<64>
type __B128 = Idx<128>
type __B256 = Idx<256>
type __B512 = Idx<512>

// ---- Internal helpers (exported like everything else; the __ prefix says "not API") ----

/// Colormap index -> plotly colorscale name (the NAMED half of the table).
/// Out-of-table indices fall back to Viridis rather than erroring: a
/// colormap is presentation, not data.
function __cmap_name(c: Int64<cmap>) -> String =
    if c == 1 then "Plasma" else if c == 2 then "Cividis" else if c == 3 then "Greys" else if c == 4 then "RdBu" else "Viridis"

/// Colormap index -> the trace's `"colorscale":` JSON VALUE: a quoted name
/// for the named half of the table, an inline stop array for 5 (Depth).
///
/// Depth's bottom stop is DISCONTINUOUS by design: position 0 is pure black
/// and position 0.001 jumps to near-black navy, so black is reserved for
/// values AT (or clipped to) the scale floor -- with a fixed [zmin, zmax]
/// range, that is the stable / never-escaped pixels -- while the slowest
/// ESCAPED values start visibly dark instead of vanishing into black.
///
/// The ramp above the floor is deliberately UNSATURATED -- blue through pale
/// blue to a warm cream, with no pure primaries and no pure white at the top.
/// A full-intensity ramp (the red/yellow/white one this replaced) is
/// exhausting on a fractal field, where high values are not a rare highlight
/// but a filigree threaded through every part of the picture.
///
/// The stops CROWD THE BOTTOM because escape-time data does. Measured on the
/// classic Mandelbrot window, 73% of pixels land in the lowest 5% of the range
/// and 91% in the lowest 10%: spaced evenly, a ramp spends almost all of its
/// colour on the few percent of pixels that escape slowly, and renders the
/// other nine-tenths as one dark mass indistinguishable from the reserved
/// black. Seven stops below the halfway mark give that crowd somewhere to go.
function __cmap_json(c: Int64<cmap>) -> String =
    if c == 5 then "[[0,\"#000000\"],[0.001,\"#16324f\"],[0.02,\"#24567f\"],[0.05,\"#3f74a3\"],[0.1,\"#6b9bc0\"],[0.2,\"#9dbdd4\"],[0.45,\"#c8d6dc\"],[1,\"#e8dcc4\"]]" else "\"" + __cmap_name(c) + "\""

// Rank-1 axis resamplers, one per budget. `na` is the SOURCE length; sample
// k reads source index (k * na) / B. The sample keeps the SOURCE's element
// type: these kernels used to end in a `* 1.0`, because a kernel reading a
// captured generic array typed its output as the LOOP INDEX (Nat<__B*>)
// rather than as what the body reads, and json_array refused it. That was a
// deduction bug (an HM-polymorphic kernel return being overwritten by the
// iterated array's element type), not a property of the resampling.

/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 16 samples.
function __ax16(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B16>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 16)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 32 samples.
function __ax32(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B32>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 32)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 64 samples.
function __ax64(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B64>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 64)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 128 samples.
function __ax128(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B128>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 128)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 256 samples.
function __ax256(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B256>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 256)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-1 axis to 512 samples.
function __ax512(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B512>) <@> lambda(k) -> a((k * na) / 512)) |> compute)

// Rank-2 grid resamplers. `hr` / `hc` are the SOURCE row / column counts;
// cell (i, j) reads z((i * hr) / B, (j * hc) / B) -- the same map the axis
// resamplers use, applied per axis, so x, y and z stay aligned.

/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 16x16 samples.
function __gr16(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B16, __B16>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 16, (j * hc) / 16)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 32x32 samples.
function __gr32(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B32, __B32>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 32, (j * hc) / 32)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 64x64 samples.
function __gr64(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B64, __B64>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 64, (j * hc) / 64)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 128x128 samples.
function __gr128(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B128, __B128>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 128, (j * hc) / 128)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 256x256 samples.
function __gr256(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B256, __B256>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 256, (j * hc) / 256)) |> compute)
/// Resample a rank-2 grid to 512x512 samples.
function __gr512(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64) -> String =
    d.json_array((method_for(range<__B512, __B512>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> z((i * hr) / 512, (j * hc) / 512)) |> compute)

/// Serialize one axis, decimated when `big` says the figure is over the cap.
/// The budget ladder is a chain of CALLS, not of inline formers: a kernel
/// former sitting directly in an `if` branch is refused by codegen (BL7004),
/// and one former per function is what sidesteps it.
function __axis_json(a: Array<T like Idx<n>>, na: Int64, big: Bool, m: Int64) -> String =
    if big then (
    if m >= 512 then __ax512(a, na) else (
    if m >= 256 then __ax256(a, na) else (
    if m >= 128 then __ax128(a, na) else (
    if m >= 64 then __ax64(a, na) else (
    if m >= 32 then __ax32(a, na) else __ax16(a, na)))))) else d.json_array(a)

/// Serialize the z grid, decimated on the same decision `__axis_json` takes,
/// with the same budget -- which is what keeps the trace rectangular.
function __grid_json(z: Array<T like Idx<nr>, Idx<nc>>, hr: Int64, hc: Int64, big: Bool, m: Int64) -> String =
    if big then (
    if m >= 512 then __gr512(z, hr, hc) else (
    if m >= 256 then __gr256(z, hr, hc) else (
    if m >= 128 then __gr128(z, hr, hc) else (
    if m >= 64 then __gr64(z, hr, hc) else (
    if m >= 32 then __gr32(z, hr, hc) else __gr16(z, hr, hc)))))) else d.json_array(z)

/// The fixed color-range fragment of a grid trace: empty -- and the trace
/// byte-identical to the pre-zmin/zmax module -- until `hi` is non-negative
/// (see the zmax marker above). Emitted with its own trailing comma so the
/// call site splices it between the trace's `"type"` entry and `xy`.
function __zrange_json(lo: Float64<zmin>, hi: Float64<zmax>) -> String =
    if hi >= 0.0 then "\"zauto\":false,\"zmin\":" + d.json_num(lo) + ",\"zmax\":" + d.json_num(hi) + "," else ""

/// The figure's layout object. Empty strings mean "no entry"; the constant
/// `"autosize":true` tail keeps the object valid JSON whatever is omitted.
/// `d.json_string` renders each label WITH its quotes, escaping whatever the
/// user put in it -- a title reading `he said "hi"` or a Windows path is an
/// ordinary label here, not a broken frame. `cam` is the camera contract
/// (see the marker above); every factory but heatmap passes "" and emits the
/// byte-identical layout it always did.
function __layout(t: String<title>, xl: String<xlabel>, yl: String<ylabel>, cam: String<camera>) -> String = {
    let tt = if t == "" then "" else "\"title\":{\"text\":" + d.json_string(t) + "},"
    let xt = if xl == "" then "" else "\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":" + d.json_string(xl) + "}},"
    let yt = if yl == "" then "" else "\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":" + d.json_string(yl) + "}},"
    let cf = if cam == "" then "" else "\"blade_camera\":{\"bindings\":" + d.json_string(cam) + "},"
    "{" + tt + xt + yt + cf + "\"autosize\":true}"
}

/// Wrap one trace + layout into a figure and emit the frame. The meta is a
/// string LITERAL (the display contract); everything runtime-varying --
/// title included -- lives in the payload's layout.
///
/// The `backend` PREFERENCE is the one thing that varies in the meta, and it
/// varies by picking between two whole literals rather than by building one:
/// `display.emit`'s meta argument must be a literal (BL5700) because the
/// frame's head and meta tail are computed ONCE at elaboration time, so even
/// `"{...\"" + "...}"` -- two literals and a `+` -- is refused. A runtime
/// `if` over two complete calls is how a runtime choice reaches a static
/// meta, the same shape the decimation ladder uses to reach a static extent.
/// The PAYLOAD is built once and shared, so the two arms differ in the meta
/// and in nothing else. Anything but 1 takes the default arm, whose meta is
/// byte-for-byte what this module emitted before the slot existed.
function __emit_fig(trace: String, layout: String, b: Int64<backend>) -> Bool = {
    let fig = "{\"data\":[" + trace + "],\"layout\":" + layout + "}"
    if b == 1 then
        d.emit("application/vnd.plotly.v1+json", fig, "{\"backend\":\"plotly\",\"preferredBackend\":\"gr\"}")
    else
        d.emit("application/vnd.plotly.v1+json", fig, "{\"backend\":\"plotly\"}")
}

/// `__emit_fig` under a STABLE id: same figure, same two meta literals, but
/// routed through `display.emit_id` so every frame sharing `pid` merges into
/// one panel entry (the plotid marker above). The id is a runtime String,
/// exactly as `stream`'s channel name is; the meta stays a literal picked by
/// a runtime `if` (BL5700), the same shape `__emit_fig` uses.
function __emit_fig_id(trace: String, layout: String, pid: String<plotid>, b: Int64<backend>) -> Bool = {
    let fig = "{\"data\":[" + trace + "],\"layout\":" + layout + "}"
    if b == 1 then
        d.emit_id("application/vnd.plotly.v1+json", pid, fig, "{\"backend\":\"plotly\",\"preferredBackend\":\"gr\"}")
    else
        d.emit_id("application/vnd.plotly.v1+json", pid, fig, "{\"backend\":\"plotly\"}")
}

// ---- Factories ----

/// Filled contour plot of z over the x/y grid. Carries the same pid/cam
/// pair as heatmap (see the plotid and camera markers): a stable id merges
/// re-emissions into one panel entry, and the camera contract makes the
/// figure a re-evaluatable view.
function contourf(x: Array<X like Idx<nx>>, y: Array<Y like Idx<ny>>, z: Array<Z like Idx<nzr>, Idx<nzc>>,
                  n: Int64<levels> = 10, c: Int64<cmap> = 0, m: Int64<maxdim> = 512,
                  lo: Float64<zmin> = 0.0, hi: Float64<zmax> = -1.0,
                  t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
                  pid: String<plotid> = "", cam: String<camera> = "",
                  sm: Int64<smoothing> = 0,
                  b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let cx = extents(x)
    let cy = extents(y)
    let big = cx > m || cy > m
    // The smooth fragment carries a SIBLING trace key through the splice
    // (the __zrange_json trick): plotly 3.7.0 keeps stroking contour lines
    // under coloring:"heatmap" despite showlines:false -- line.width 0 is
    // the knob that actually silences them (verified against the vendored
    // renderer).
    let contours = if sm == 1 then "{\"coloring\":\"heatmap\",\"showlines\":false},\"line\":{\"width\":0}" else "{\"coloring\":\"fill\"}"
    let xy = "\"x\":" + __axis_json(x, cx, big, m) + ",\"y\":" + __axis_json(y, cy, big, m) + ",\"z\":" + __grid_json(z, cy, cx, big, m)
    let trace = "{\"type\":\"contour\"," + __zrange_json(lo, hi) + xy + ",\"colorscale\":" + __cmap_json(c) + ",\"contours\":" + contours + ",\"ncontours\":" + d.json_num(n) + "}"
    let lay = __layout(t, xl, yl, cam)
    if pid == "" then __emit_fig(trace, lay, b) else __emit_fig_id(trace, lay, pid, b)
}

/// Line contour plot of z over the x/y grid.
function contour(x: Array<X like Idx<nx>>, y: Array<Y like Idx<ny>>, z: Array<Z like Idx<nzr>, Idx<nzc>>,
                 n: Int64<levels> = 10, c: Int64<cmap> = 0, m: Int64<maxdim> = 512,
                 lo: Float64<zmin> = 0.0, hi: Float64<zmax> = -1.0,
                 t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
                 b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let cx = extents(x)
    let cy = extents(y)
    let big = cx > m || cy > m
    let xy = "\"x\":" + __axis_json(x, cx, big, m) + ",\"y\":" + __axis_json(y, cy, big, m) + ",\"z\":" + __grid_json(z, cy, cx, big, m)
    let trace = "{\"type\":\"contour\"," + __zrange_json(lo, hi) + xy + ",\"colorscale\":" + __cmap_json(c) + ",\"contours\":{\"coloring\":\"lines\"},\"ncontours\":" + d.json_num(n) + "}"
    __emit_fig(trace, __layout(t, xl, yl, ("" : camera)), b)
}

/// Heatmap of z over the x/y grid. The `pid`/`cam` pair (see the plotid and
/// camera markers) makes a heatmap RE-EVALUATABLE: a stable id merges every
/// re-emission into one panel entry, and the camera contract tells a viewer
/// which session bindings position the view. Both default off, and off is
/// byte-identical to the pre-slot module.
function heatmap(x: Array<X like Idx<nx>>, y: Array<Y like Idx<ny>>, z: Array<Z like Idx<nzr>, Idx<nzc>>,
                 c: Int64<cmap> = 0, m: Int64<maxdim> = 512,
                 lo: Float64<zmin> = 0.0, hi: Float64<zmax> = -1.0,
                 t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
                 pid: String<plotid> = "", cam: String<camera> = "",
                 sm: Int64<smoothing> = 0,
                 b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let cx = extents(x)
    let cy = extents(y)
    let big = cx > m || cy > m
    let xy = "\"x\":" + __axis_json(x, cx, big, m) + ",\"y\":" + __axis_json(y, cy, big, m) + ",\"z\":" + __grid_json(z, cy, cx, big, m)
    // Smooth mode renders the raster with bilinear interpolation (plotly
    // zsmooth "best") -- the continuous look without contour GEOMETRY: a
    // contour pass over a fractal field generates polygon soup (measured
    // ~700x the raster's render time in the panel), which is why the
    // re-evaluatable views ride heatmap.
    let zs = if sm == 1 then ",\"zsmooth\":\"best\"" else ""
    let trace = "{\"type\":\"heatmap\"," + __zrange_json(lo, hi) + xy + ",\"colorscale\":" + __cmap_json(c) + zs + "}"
    let lay = __layout(t, xl, yl, cam)
    if pid == "" then __emit_fig(trace, lay, b) else __emit_fig_id(trace, lay, pid, b)
}

/// Line plot of y against x.
function line(x: X^1, y: Y^1,
              t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
              b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let trace = "{\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"x\":" + d.json_array(x) + ",\"y\":" + d.json_array(y) + "}"
    __emit_fig(trace, __layout(t, xl, yl, ("" : camera)), b)
}

/// Scatter plot of y against x.
function scatter(x: X^1, y: Y^1,
                 t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
                 b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let trace = "{\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"x\":" + d.json_array(x) + ",\"y\":" + d.json_array(y) + "}"
    __emit_fig(trace, __layout(t, xl, yl, ("" : camera)), b)
}

// ---- Live plot streaming --------------------------------------------------
//
// `plot.stream(name, x, y)` is the OTHER kind of frame this module emits: not
// a finished figure but one INSTALMENT of a chart that a long-running cell
// keeps extending. Three things separate it from the five factories above.
//
//   * ITS OWN MIME, `application/vnd.blade.plotstream.v1+json`, carrying a
//     backend-neutral `{channel, epoch, x, y, labels}` object rather than a
//     plotly figure. The renderer assembles the figure; the program ships
//     data. That is what lets the panel APPEND to a trace it already has
//     instead of repainting a whole new one per batch.
//   * ITS OWN IDENTITY. `display.emit_id` puts the CHANNEL NAME in the
//     frame's `meta.id`, so every frame of one channel merges into one chart
//     -- stable across calls, across a REPL session replay, and independent
//     of how many other plots ran first (an ordinal id would drift the moment
//     an earlier cell gained a plot). The channel name is also carried inside
//     the payload, so a consumer that never looks at `meta` still knows which
//     series it has.
//   * NO DECIMATION. The grid factories resample because a figure is a
//     one-shot payload sized by the source data; a stream frame is sized by
//     the program's own batch, and the RENDERER owns thinning the assembled
//     series. Decimating here would throw away samples the renderer is about
//     to need.
//
// During an `ide serve` evaluation each stream frame is forwarded the instant
// it is produced (Blade.Display.Frame's sink -> the `{"event":"display"}`
// line), which is what makes a training curve move while the cell is still
// running. Everywhere else -- `blade run`, `blade repl`, the corpus -- it is
// an ordinary sentinel line on stdout, byte-identical in both lanes.

/// The optional-label tail of a stream payload. An empty label is OMITTED
/// rather than sent empty, exactly as `__layout` omits an empty axis title:
/// "not given" and "given as blank" are the same request, and the shorter
/// frame is the one that repeats thousands of times in a training loop. Each
/// fragment leads with its own comma because the payload's required fields
/// always precede it.
function __stream_labels(t: String<title>, xl: String<xlabel>, yl: String<ylabel>) -> String = {
    let tt = if t == "" then "" else ",\"title\":" + d.json_string(t)
    let xt = if xl == "" then "" else ",\"xlabel\":" + d.json_string(xl)
    let yt = if yl == "" then "" else ",\"ylabel\":" + d.json_string(yl)
    tt + xt + yt
}

/// One frame of the live chart named `name`, carrying the samples `y` against
/// `x`. Frames sharing a `name` are instalments of ONE chart.
///
/// The `backend` slot is the same preference hint the factories carry, and it
/// moves the meta and nothing else -- see the PREFERRED BACKEND note in the
/// module header. Two whole literals picked by a runtime `if`, because
/// `display.emit_id`'s meta argument is an elaboration-time literal (BL5700)
/// exactly like `display.emit`'s.
function stream(name: String, x: X^1, y: Y^1,
                e: Int64<epoch> = -1,
                t: String<title> = "", xl: String<xlabel> = "", yl: String<ylabel> = "",
                b: Int64<backend> = 0) -> Bool = {
    let payload = "{\"channel\":" + d.json_string(name) + ",\"epoch\":" + d.json_num(e) + ",\"x\":" + d.json_array(x) + ",\"y\":" + d.json_array(y) + __stream_labels(t, xl, yl) + "}"
    if b == 1 then
        d.emit_id("application/vnd.blade.plotstream.v1+json", name, payload, "{\"stream\":true,\"backend\":\"plotly\",\"preferredBackend\":\"gr\"}")
    else
        d.emit_id("application/vnd.blade.plotstream.v1+json", name, payload, "{\"stream\":true,\"backend\":\"plotly\"}")
}
